{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"As of November 6, 2026:\n\n- **Rule 6c-11 covers** ETFs organized as registered open-end management investment companies, whether index-tracking or actively managed, provided they satisfy daily portfolio transparency and the rule’s other operational, disclosure and recordkeeping conditions.\n- **Leveraged and inverse ETFs can also rely on 6c-11**; they are no longer categorically excluded. They must comply with applicable **Rule 18f-4** requirements, including derivatives-risk management and VaR-based leverage limits. Limited grandfathering accommodates certain existing funds targeting returns exceeding 200% of an index. This is not blanket permission for unlimited leverage.\n- **Separate exemptive relief is needed** for unit-investment-trust ETFs, nontransparent/semitransparent active ETFs, and ETF share classes of mutual funds.\n- Commodity pools and grantor-trust exchange-traded products fall outside 6c-11’s registered-fund framework.\n\n**Worth knowing:** Rule 6c-11 addresses ETF operation; Rule 18f-4 separately governs derivatives use and related leverage risk."}}
